 "upload": {
  "title": "Russia Rations Fuel as Ukraine's Drones Hit Refineries #shorts",
  "description": "Two industry sources told Reuters on November 10 that renewed Ukrainian drone strikes since late October have reduced Russia's oil refining capacity by about 30%, disrupting nine of Russia's 35 major refineries. Leningrad Governor Alexander Drozdenko said a Ukrainian drone attack set the Kirishi refinery, Russia's second-largest, on fire on Monday, November 9. Euronews, Semafor and the Kyiv Independent report that Rosneft has capped fuel sales in several Russian regions, at 30 litres per purchase in cities; Rosneft had eased rationing in late October during a pause in Ukraine's strikes. Overnight into November 11, Drozdenko said 146 drones were downed over the Leningrad region and a fire broke out in the Ust-Luga port zone; Reuters said it could not verify the drone count. Lithuania and Latvia warned residents to take shelter over unidentified drones, according to reports. US-mediated Russia-Ukraine talks in Abu Dhabi were set to continue on November 11. The 30% figure comes from anonymous industry sources and has not been officially confirmed.\n\nSources:\nReuters via Kyiv Independent and Euromaidan Press, Nov 10, 2026: Ukrainian strikes cut Russia's oil refining capacity by 30%\nAl Jazeera, Nov 9, 2026: At least four people killed as Russia and Ukraine trade strikes overnight\nEuronews, Nov 9, 2026: Ukraine hits Russia's second-largest refinery as fuel rationing spreads\nSemafor, Nov 9, 2026: Russia rations fuel as Ukraine resumes strikes on refineries\nAl Jazeera, Nov 11, 2026: Russia claims interception of 146 drones in Leningrad region\nTASS, Nov 11, 2026: Fire breaks out after drone attack in Ust-Luga port zone\n\n#geopolitics #Russia #Ukraine #Oil",
